# Closed two different ways depending on which alias form it is, per the
# review's own suggested direction:
#   - The ONE-SHOT `-c alias.*=` / `--config-env alias.*=...` form (a) is
#     REJECTED OUTRIGHT, unconditionally, regardless of what the alias's
#     value actually is: a legitimate dispatch-pipeline command line has no
#     reason to define an ad-hoc alias inline, so there is no case where
#     honoring one is necessary, and the value could itself be a shell alias
#     (`!...`) or something else not safe to statically evaluate --
#     resolving and evaluating it correctly in every case is a strictly
#     harder and riskier problem than simply refusing to run any command
#     line that defines one. See _interactive_guard_git_find_subcommand()'s
#     new `_interactive_guard_git_oneshot_alias` detection and the git
#     wrapper script's hard-reject check in _lib.sh.
#   - PERSISTENT aliases (b) (an `[alias]` entry already in ~/.gitconfig, or
#     a `gh alias set` entry already in gh's config) ARE resolved -- one
#     level -- by asking the REAL git/gh binary what it actually knows via
#     `git config --get-regexp '^alias\.'` / `gh alias list`, exactly as the
#     review's suggested direction describes, rather than rejecting every
#     unrecognized subcommand token outright (which would make ordinary,
#     harmless aliases like a `co = checkout` shortcut unusable in an
#     interactive shell for no security benefit). If the resolved token IS
#     "push" (git) or the resolved argv IS a `pr merge`/merge-API call (gh),
#     it is evaluated exactly as if the caller had typed that directly. See
#     _interactive_guard_git_resolve_persistent_alias() and
#     _interactive_guard_gh_resolve_persistent_alias() in _lib.sh.
# Both are verified with dedicated, passing regression-test scenarios, not
# just prose -- see tests/interactive_session_guard_test.sh's "ROUND 5"
# scenarios ("BYPASS VECTOR 10"), which reproduce the exact command lines
# from the round-4 review and prove they are now BLOCKED.
#
# Two residual gaps were found while building and testing this round's fix
# and are disclosed honestly below rather than silently omitted (see KNOWN
# LIMITATIONS): persistent-alias resolution only follows ONE level (a
# nested alias whose value is itself another alias name is not further
# resolved), and a gh SHELL alias (an expansion beginning with `!`, an
# arbitrary shell command rather than a gh subcommand token) is left
# unresolved by this guard's OWN alias-resolution logic specifically -- both
# are proven, not just claimed, by dedicated regression-test scenarios that
# show the current, honest behavior rather than asserting a stronger
# guarantee than what was actually built.
#
# MECHANISM (three signals, all required):
#   1. INVOCATION_ID -- the real environment variable systemd sets for every
#      unit it launches (present for veridian-worker@*.service /
#      veridian-supervisor@*.service units spawned by scripts/veridian-task.py's
#      systemd --user integration). Absent in a normal interactive login/SSH
#      shell. On its own this is just a plain env var read -- trivially
#      spoofed by `export INVOCATION_ID=anything` -- so it is never trusted
#      alone (see ROUND 2 above).
#   2. This process's real, kernel-tracked cgroup membership (read from
#      /proc/self/cgroup) actually names a veridian-worker@*/
#      veridian-supervisor@* systemd unit. Cgroup membership is assigned by
#      the kernel when systemd forks the unit's process tree and is
#      inherited by every descendant process -- there is no environment
#      variable or shell builtin that lets an ordinary interactive process
#      rewrite its own cgroup path to impersonate a unit it isn't actually
#      running under. A real interactive SSH/login shell's cgroup names
#      `session-N.scope`/`user@<uid>.service`, never a veridian-worker/
#      veridian-supervisor unit.
#   3. [ROUND 4] The task_id parsed out of that matching cgroup unit name
#      (the text between `@` and `.service`) names a REAL, currently
#      in_progress task: /opt/veridian/ai-os/tasks/<task_id>/task.yaml
#      exists and its `status:` field reads `in_progress`. Signal 2 alone
#      answers "does this process's cgroup carry the right NAME PATTERN" --
#      it does not answer "does that name correspond to something real",
#      and ROUND 4's audit could not rule out an ordinary interactive
#      process using `systemd-run --user --unit=veridian-worker@<fake>.service`
#      to obtain a genuinely kernel-tracked cgroup with a name matching
#      signal 2's pattern but no real dispatched work behind it (see ROUND 4
#      above for what was and was not proven live about that specific
#      attempt). Cross-referencing against the real, live task registry
#      closes that gap regardless of how a matching-but-fake cgroup was
#      produced: a `systemd-run`-forged unit's instance name is whatever the
#      caller typed (e.g. `fake-test`), which has no corresponding, real,
#      in_progress entry under /opt/veridian/ai-os/tasks/ -- a genuine
#      dispatched worker's instance name always does, because
#      scripts/veridian-task.py names the unit after the real task_id it
#      just created the task directory for.
# The command is only treated as "real dispatched worker context" when ALL
# THREE signals agree; any one or two alone are not enough.
#
# SCOPE: only a PR merge (`gh pr merge`, or `gh api -X PUT|PATCH` against a
# `.../pulls/<n>/merge` path) and `git push` to a protected branch (or
# `push --all`/`--mirror`, which reach protected branches regardless of the
# current branch) are intercepted. Every other gh/git subcommand (view,
# checks, pr list, pr comment, pr diff, run rerun, log, diff, status, push to
# a non-protected/feature branch, `gh api` calls that are not a merge, etc.)
# passes through to the real binary unchanged in both contexts -- this guard
# never blocks read-only verification/status-checking work.
#
# KNOWN LIMITATIONS (honest, not silently glossed over):
#   - Same-account limitation (unchanged from round 1): this guard is scoped
#     to the AI session's own discipline, NOT a security boundary against the
